---
/**
 * Act two - phases.md 8.4, "the promise": the chronological feed.
 *
 * **The device mechanism moved here from the hero** (owner, 2026-09-03), mechanically
 * unchanged: sticky, grows on scroll, feed advances one release per further scroll - see
 * `promise.ts`.
 *
 * **There is no hand-off cover, and growth happens on the way in (owner, 2026-09-03).**
 * The section used to open under a `.blackout` layer that faded off over ~200px, which is
 * what read as the phone fading in slowly; the device is now simply visible, at full
 * opacity, from the first frame it is on screen. Growth no longer costs the pin any
 * scroll either: it runs across the section's *approach* - starting as the section (and
 * so the phone) comes into the viewport, finishing exactly when the section's top reaches
 * the viewport's top and the pin engages. `promise.ts` drives `--feed-p` from
 * `observeScrollProgress`'s `entry`, not from its `progress`, which is what makes that
 * possible; the pin's own scroll now belongs entirely to the feed, its rest and the outro.
 *
 * **The device rests near the top from frame one** (owner, 2026-09-03, revised from an
 * initial pass that rested it low with a lot of empty space above it): `--rest-top` and
 * `--end-top` (the grown, centred position) are both ~6svh, so growth is almost entirely
 * a scale change rather than a rise - there is nowhere for whitespace to hide.
 *
 * **The cyan atmosphere waits for the first advance** (owner, 2026-09-03): it does not
 * exist in the hero's version of this mechanism at all, and appearing from frame one read
 * as arriving too early. `--feed-atmosphere` ramps 0→1 across the *first* feed advance
 * only (`promise.ts`), then stays lit - "after the first feed item has been scrolled".
 *
 * **The outro (owner, 2026-09-03, keeping what first shipped as a bug):** after the feed's
 * trailing rest, the device settles smaller (70vh - `--settle-scale`, a ratio of `--end-h`
 * the same way `--rest-scale` already is) while staying on screen, and the section's own
 * copy fades in beneath it with a fixed 20svh gap to the bottom of the viewport - both
 * still inside the one sticky pin, not a separate unpinned block below it.
 *
 * **`FEED_LIMIT` raised from 2 to 3** (owner, 2026-09-03) - `showcase.ts` owns the count,
 * shared with `Problem.astro`'s independent chip call.
 *
 * **Cyan bloom** (`design-system.md` §2, "clarity/order") via `BloomField act="feed"`,
 * already defined at 8.1 and unused until now.
 *
 * **`#feed` is act one's CTA target** (owner, 2026-09-03). "See how it works" now lives on
 * `Problem.astro` and points here, at the full-screen phone. The anchor lands on the
 * section's top, which - since growth is driven by the *approach* - is exactly the moment
 * the phone reaches full size, so the jump arrives on a composed frame rather than mid-tween.
 *
 * No device mockup elsewhere claims to *be* the app (`design-system.md` §6) - this
 * section is where that claim now lives exclusively, since the hero no longer makes it.
 */
import BloomField from '../BloomField.astro';
import Device from '../device/Device.astro';
import FeedScreen from '../device/FeedScreen.astro';
import { getFeedReleases } from '../device/showcase';

/**
 * How many times the feed advances, and therefore how much scroll the feed track is
 * given. Read from the same module the feed renders from, so the number of releases on
 * screen and the scroll allocated to them cannot disagree.
 */
const showcase = await getFeedReleases();
const advances = showcase.length - 1;

/**
 * The chronology stamps beside the phone - one per release, split so the day can be set at
 * display scale with the month and year small beneath it (`Gate A`, option A, approved by
 * the owner 2026-09-03). `en-GB` and `UTC` match `ReleaseCard.astro` exactly, so the stamp
 * and the date inside the phone can never disagree about what day a release came out.
 */
const stamps = showcase.map((release) => {
  const at = new Date(`${release.data.releaseDate}T00:00:00Z`);
  return {
    day: new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', { day: '2-digit', timeZone: 'UTC' }).format(at),
    month: new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', { month: 'short', timeZone: 'UTC' }).format(at),
    year: new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', { year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' }).format(at),
  };
});

import { catalogue } from '../../i18n';

const t = catalogue(Astro.currentLocale);
---

<section id="feed" class="feed" data-feed-stage style={`--advances: ${advances}`}>
  <div class="atmosphere">
    <BloomField act="feed" intensity="feature" />
  </div>

  <div class="pin" data-feed-pin>
    {
      /*
      Act two's decorative anchor (Gate A option A, owner 2026-09-03): the release's own date
      set at display scale in the margin beside the phone, swapping as the feed pages.

      `reference-study.md` §3 - superlist's pricing page, where "the page's decorative anchor
      is display type itself" and the fun "comes entirely from scale, colour, and typographic
      wit". Deliberately *not* their execution: no italic, no crossing glyph, and it is a
      real fact about the release rather than a wordmark.

      Alternates margins, and each one swipes: in from below, out towards the top (owner,
      2026-09-04). Both are `promise.ts`'s `renderStamps` - the same paged position the feed
      itself rides, so the date in the margin and the release on screen cannot disagree.

      `aria-hidden`, and this is the honest call rather than a convenience: it restates a date
      the device already shows, so `design-system.md` §6's rule that a screen reader must never
      have to walk a fake app applies to it exactly as it does to the phone.

      Hidden below 1024px, where the phone leaves no margin to put it in.
    */
    }
    <div class="chronology" aria-hidden="true">
      {
        stamps.map((stamp, index) => (
          /*
           * `--i` is the stamp's place in the list, used only below `md`, where the dates
           * are a plain column laid out down the section at a fixed step rather than one
           * crossfading element (8.14c Gate C, owner: "completely normally placed and
           * scrolled - not animated, as if there's no js to them ... like a calendar").
           * The opacity below is the desktop build's starting state and is overridden at
           * that width.
           */
          <span class="stamp" data-stamp style={`--i: ${index}; opacity: ${index === 0 ? 1 : 0}`}>
            <span class="day">{stamp.day}</span>
            <span class="rest">
              <span class="month">{stamp.month}</span> <span class="year">{stamp.year}</span>
            </span>
          </span>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <div class="device-slot">
      <Device>
        <FeedScreen />
      </Device>
    </div>

    <div class="copy">
      <h2 class="headline">{t.home.feed.headline}</h2>
      <p class="sub">{t.home.feed.sub}</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!--
    Deliberately `releases`, not `feed`: Astro scopes this component's styles by adding one
    shared `astro-cid` to *every* element in it, so a modifier class that happens to match
    the section's own class is selected by the section's own rule. As `travel feed` this
    spacer picked up `.feed`'s `background` and `position: relative` and became a
    1152px-tall opaque black block sitting in flow above the sticky `.atmosphere` - the
    slab the owner saw scrolling past behind the phone (2026-09-03).
  -->
  <!--
    8.14c: the scroll the phone spends moving aside, before a single release advances.
    Zero-height at every width from 768 up, where the phone does not move aside at all -
    the element is inert on desktop rather than conditional, so both branches read the same
    spacers and nothing has to know which one is running.
  -->
  <div class="travel shift" data-feed-shift></div>
  <div class="travel releases" data-feed-feed-travel></div>
  <div class="travel rest" data-feed-rest></div>
  <div class="travel settle" data-feed-settle></div>
  <!--
    The outro's trailing hold (owner, 2026-09-03): the total after the feed is unchanged,
    but the settle now finishes inside the first two-thirds of it and this spacer is the
    rest, so the composition is seen standing rather than arriving and immediately leaving.
    It carries no hook of its own and `promise.ts` never reads it - `settleProgress` is
    measured against `.travel.settle` alone and simply clamps at 1 across this one.
  -->
  <div class="travel outro-hold"></div>
</section>

<style>
  @property --feed-p {
    syntax: '<number>';
    inherits: true;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  /* The outro's own progress, 0 (still full size, feed just ended) to 1 (settled). */
  @property --feed-settle {
    syntax: '<number>';
    inherits: true;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  /* How lit the cyan atmosphere is, 0 to 1 - ramps in only once the whole feed has
   * finished advancing. */
  @property --feed-atmosphere {
    syntax: '<number>';
    inherits: true;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  /*
   * 8.14c, mobile only. How far the phone has moved aside: 0 centred and full size, 1
   * shrunk and pushed right with 30% of it past the edge, the dates standing in the space
   * it left. It rises across `.travel.shift`, holds through every advance, and is brought
   * back down by `--feed-return` - one signal for "the phone is out of the way", so the
   * dates cannot be lit while the phone is centred over them.
   */
  @property --feed-aside {
    syntax: '<number>';
    inherits: true;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  /* The close: the phone coming back to the middle and the copy arriving under it. */
  @property --feed-return {
    syntax: '<number>';
    inherits: true;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  .feed {
    position: relative;
    display: flow-root;
    overflow-x: clip;
    background: var(--color-ink-950);
    --end-h: var(--device-end-h);
    --rest-scale: 0.659;
    --advance: 64svh;
    --rest-top: 6svh;
    /* 70vh against an 88svh end - same "express it as the ratio" discipline as
     * `--rest-scale`, so it holds whichever term of `--end-h`'s `min()` wins. Shared with
     * act three since 8.5's Gate B: act three opens on the phone at exactly the size this
     * section leaves it, so the two must read one number, not two equal ones. */
    --settle-scale: var(--device-settle-scale);
  }

  /*
   * `.atmosphere` and `.pin` are two stacked `position: sticky` layers with
   * a negative margin, inside a section that is thousands of pixels tall. Each gets its
   * own forced, correctly-bounded compositing layer (`transform: translateZ(0)` +
   * `contain: paint`) - a defensive measure against a reported rendering artifact (a stray
   * dark/black rectangle, and dimming bleeding onto fixed page chrome above these layers)
   * that could not be reproduced in either Chromium or WebKit via Playwright, so is
   * suspected to be a GPU-compositing tiling bug specific to hardware-accelerated
   * rendering on a very tall sticky ancestor. `isolation: isolate` on each also guarantees
   * their stacking (and any blend mode inside `.atmosphere`) cannot affect anything
   * outside their own box, including the fixed header.
   */
  /*
   * **The overlap is a negative `margin-top` on `.pin`, not a negative `margin-bottom`
   * here** (owner, 8.5's Gate C). A sticky element's constraint rectangle is its
   * containing block inset by the element's *own* margins, so `margin-bottom: -100svh`
   * on this layer pushed the bottom of that rectangle a full viewport *below* the
   * section - and the atmosphere therefore stayed stuck to the screen for 900px after
   * `.pin` had released and scrolled away, riding over the top of act three. Measured at
   * 1440x900: at 300px past the release the pin was at -300 and this layer was still at
   * 0. Moving the same negative margin to the next sibling's top gives the identical flow
   * position and the identical overlap while leaving both constraint rectangles equal to
   * the section, so the two layers now release on the same pixel (verified: delta 0 at
   * the release and at every offset past it).
   */
  .atmosphere {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    height: 100svh;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: calc(0.55 * var(--feed-atmosphere));
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(72% 58% at 50% 52%, #000 0%, transparent 74%);
    mask-image: radial-gradient(72% 58% at 50% 52%, #000 0%, transparent 74%);
    isolation: isolate;
    contain: paint;
    transform: translateZ(0);
  }

  .pin {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    height: 100svh;
    /* Pulls this layer back over `.atmosphere`, which now keeps its own 100svh of flow.
     * See the note there for why the negative margin lives on this side of the pair. */
    margin-top: -100svh;
    z-index: 1;
    overflow: hidden;
    isolation: isolate;
    contain: paint;
    transform: translateZ(0);
  }

  .travel.releases {
    height: calc(var(--advances) * var(--advance));
  }

  /* A trailing hold on the last release, so it is seen at rest rather than only in
   * passing - same reasoning as the hero's own former `.travel.rest`. Shortened from
   * `1.12` at rev 24 to lend the settle its own room; see below. */
  .travel.rest {
    height: calc(var(--advance) * 0.8);
  }

  /*
   * Rev 24 (owner: the outro copy "still seems to appear too abruptly"). The onset curve
   * was already near-flat, so the remaining abruptness was simply distance: 32svh is not
   * much scroll for a full arrival. The settle grows to 52svh and the trailing hold on the
   * last release gives back the same ~20svh, so the total after the feed is unchanged -
   * 51 + 52 + 18 against the previous 72 + 32 + 18. Lengthening the settle rather than
   * decoupling the copy from it keeps the device's shrink and the copy's arrival on one
   * signal, which is what stops them drifting apart.
   */
  .travel.settle {
    height: 52svh;
  }

  .travel.outro-hold {
    height: 18svh;
  }

  .device-slot {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: var(--rest-top);
    height: var(--end-h);
    aspect-ratio: 390 / 844;
    --end-top: calc((100svh - var(--end-h)) / 2);
    transform-origin: 50% 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* Always fully opaque (owner, 2026-09-03) - the device never fades, and nothing covers
     * it any more either: it is simply on screen, growing, from the first frame of the
     * section's approach. */
    /* Growth (`--feed-p`) and the outro (`--feed-settle`) are two separate, sequential
     * multipliers on the same scale - growth never has to know the outro exists, and vice
     * versa. Position (`top`) does not change for the outro: shrinking in place, top held
     * fixed, is what reads as "pushed up" without an extra translate to keep in sync. */
    transform: translateX(-50%) translateY(calc((var(--end-top) - var(--rest-top)) * var(--feed-p)))
      scale(
        calc(
          (var(--rest-scale) + (1 - var(--rest-scale)) * var(--feed-p)) *
            (1 - var(--feed-settle) * (1 - var(--settle-scale)))
        )
      );
    will-change: transform;
  }

  /*
   * The stamps are stacked in one place and crossfaded, rather than laid out in a column
   * and slid: `promise.ts` already owns a paged, threshold-driven position for the feed and
   * drives these from that same number, so they can never drift out of step with the
   * release actually on screen. A second scroll mapping is exactly how they would.
   *
   * Appears with the phone (`--feed-p`) and leaves with the outro (`--feed-settle`), so it
   * never competes with the copy that lands over the device at the end.
   */
  .chronology {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: calc(var(--feed-p) * (1 - var(--feed-settle)));
  }

  /*
   * Each stamp fills one margin beside the phone and alternates which one (owner,
   * 2026-09-04). The width is the margin itself - half the pin, less half the grown
   * device's own width, which its `--end-h` and the 390/844 aspect give exactly - so the
   * stamp can never overlap the phone at any viewport.
   *
   * `nth-child` rather than an index written into the markup: the alternation is a fact
   * about position in the list, and the list is the only thing that decides it.
   */
  .stamp {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    width: calc(50% - var(--end-h) * 0.5 * 390 / 844);
    display: grid;
    align-content: center;
    justify-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-1);
    color: var(--color-plum-500);
    /* `transform` as well as `opacity` now: the swipe below is scrubbed, and this element
     * is repainted on every frame of an advance without it. */
    will-change: opacity, transform;
  }

  .stamp:nth-child(odd) {
    left: 0;
  }

  .stamp:nth-child(even) {
    right: 0;
  }

  .day {
    font-size: clamp(3.5rem, 7vw, 7rem);
    line-height: 0.9;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    /* Quiet enough to read as atmosphere behind the product rather than as a second
     * headline competing with the one that arrives at the end. */
    opacity: 0.32;
  }

  .rest {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-mute-200);
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    .chronology {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  .copy {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 20svh;
    z-index: 2;
    max-width: var(--content-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--gutter);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--spacing-4);
    justify-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    /*
     * Smoothstep, not the raw signal (owner, 2026-09-03: the text "fades in way too
     * suddenly. It's like it pops"). `--feed-settle` is flat 0 through the whole feed and
     * its rest and then starts climbing linearly, so the copy went from absent to visibly
     * present within a few pixels of scroll - the abruptness was in the onset, not the
     * length. Rev 24 goes further still, to smootherstep (`6t⁵ − 15t⁴ + 10t³`), whose first
     * *two* derivatives vanish at each end - the copy leaves and reaches full presence with
     * no detectable moment of starting. Written out rather than eased in JS because both
     * properties below must ride the identical curve; a second, separately-eased signal is
     * how the fade and the drift would desynchronise.
     */
    --settle-ease: calc(
      var(--feed-settle) * var(--feed-settle) * var(--feed-settle) *
        (var(--feed-settle) * (var(--feed-settle) * 6 - 15) + 10)
    );
    opacity: var(--settle-ease);
    transform: translateY(calc((1 - var(--settle-ease)) * var(--spacing-4)));
    /*
     * The outro lands this copy *on top of* the settled device - by design, but the phone's
     * own track title and transport controls sit exactly where the headline crosses it, and
     * white-on-plum against white-on-black gave the two nothing to separate them (owner,
     * 2026-09-03). Three stacked shadows rather than one: a tight dark edge that keeps the
     * letterforms crisp against the screen's lit areas, and two progressively wider, softer
     * pools that darken the artwork behind the whole line so it reads as a layer above the
     * device rather than printed onto it.
     *
     * `text-shadow`, not a scrim behind `.copy`: a rectangle of darkness would cut a visible
     * edge across the phone, which is the exact artifact this section just spent a round
     * removing. Inherited from `.copy` so the headline and sub-line cannot drift apart. It
     * is static - `design-system.md` §6 bans *animating* a shadow in a scrubbed timeline,
     * and nothing here animates it; only the element's own opacity moves.
     */
    text-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85),
      0 4px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75),
      0 0 48px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);
    /*
     * **Load-bearing, and measured.** Without it this element is re-rastered on every frame
     * of the outro, because its `opacity` is animating and a blurred `text-shadow` over
     * display type is expensive to paint - a trace across the outro at 1512×945 @2x put
     * `RasterTask` at **460ms with the shadow against 5ms without**, which is what the owner
     * reported as the site lagging the moment the text appears (2026-09-03). Promoting the
     * element paints the shadow once into its own layer and leaves `opacity` and `transform`
     * as compositor-only work: the same trace then reads **8ms**. The frame-cadence probes
     * that ran first showed a clean 60fps in every variant and found nothing - raster does
     * not block the main thread's rAF here, so only the trace exposed it.
     */
    will-change: transform;
  }

  .headline {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure-display);
    font-size: var(--text-3xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-display);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-white);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .sub {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-body);
    color: var(--color-mute-200);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /*
   * The designed reduced-motion composition (acceptance-criteria.md §4): no travel, no
   * cover - the device settles straight to its outro size and the copy is simply present
   * beneath it.
   */
  /* At `md` and up only since 8.14c round 8 (owner): below it, phones keep the full
   * motion build under Reduce Motion. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) and (min-width: 768px) {
    .feed {
      --advance: 0svh;
    }

    .travel.settle,
    .travel.outro-hold {
      height: 0svh;
    }

    .pin {
      position: relative;
      height: 100svh;
      /* `.atmosphere` is taken out of flow below, so there is no longer 100svh of
       * sibling above this to pull back over. */
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    .atmosphere {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
      height: auto;
    }

    .device-slot {
      will-change: auto;
    }
  }

  /*
   * 8.14c: below `md` the pin is gone, and the composition inside it is rebuilt rather
   * than merely unpinned (Gate C, owner). Two things change and they are the same change:
   * **the copy comes off the phone's screen, and the phone stops resizing.**
   *
   * Everything above this block is absolutely positioned inside a viewport-height pin,
   * because at 1440 that is a deliberate outro - the copy lands *over* a device the scrub
   * has already shrunk to `--settle-scale`, with a triple `text-shadow` earning its
   * legibility. None of that survives at 390: the phone is nearly the whole frame, so the
   * same overlay put the headline across the track title and the transport controls.
   *
   * So the pin becomes an ordinary column - phone, then copy beneath it - and the device
   * is sized for a viewport it has to share. `--mobile-device-h` is 60svh against the
   * `min(88svh, 195vw)` of `--device-end-h`, which resolves to ~743px here and leaves
   * nothing for the words.
   *
   * `--feed-settle` is therefore driven to 0 rather than 1 (`promise.ts`): the shrink
   * existed to make room for copy that is no longer there to need it.
   */
  /*
   * 8.14c Gate C, round 2 - **only the phone is pinned** (owner), and that one sentence is
   * the whole structure. Above `md` the entire `.pin` is the sticky element and everything
   * inside it is pinned together. Here the phone itself is sticky and `.pin` is an ordinary
   * tall block, so the dates and the copy are plain flow content scrolling past it with no
   * JavaScript touching them at all - "as if there's no js to them ... like a calendar".
   *
   * What that buys, beyond matching the brief: the dates cannot drift out of step with the
   * release on screen by being mis-timed, because they are not timed. `promise.ts` reads
   * which date is level with the phone and asks the feed for that release, so the column is
   * the source of truth and the feed follows it.
   */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .feed {
      /*
       * Room between act one and the phone (owner, round 3: "too close to the previous
       * section, double the margin"). Act one ends with ~124px of its own empty lower half
       * below its CTA and the phone began immediately after it, so this is that perceived
       * margin doubled, then halved at round 4 and halved again at round 5 (owner, both
       * times: "cut the top padding by 50%").
       *
       * It is deliberately **not** the 300px that used to sit here. That read as "a huge
       * gap" when it was a margin painting the page's plum ground between two ink-950
       * sections; as padding inside this section it is simply dark, and the distance that
       * felt wrong before was the colour, not entirely the length.
       *
       * Removed outright at 8.14c round 5 (owner: "no margin on the top so it's closer to
       * the copy").
       */
      padding-top: 0;

      /*
       * Bigger in both states (owner, round 2) - was 60svh, and shrinking barely at all
       * when it goes aside. `--device-end-h` (~743px here) is a phone with nothing beside
       * it, which is what this width cannot afford.
       *
       * **Sized from the width, capped by the height** (round 5). It was 78svh, so its width
       * - and with it the whitespace left of the aside phone, `100vw - 0.9 * width` - followed
       * the screen's *height*: 28% of the width on a 412x915 phone, which the owner approved,
       * but 42% on a 375x667 one. 80vw wide is exactly the 412x915 composition on every
       * phone. The 90svh cap is what stops a short, wide screen pushing the phone under the
       * header and off the bottom edge; below that aspect the whitespace grows again, but
       * from 34% at 375x667 rather than 42%.
       */
      --mobile-device-h: min(calc(80vw * 844 / 390), 90svh);
      --phone-w: calc(var(--mobile-device-h) * 390 / 844);
      /* Round 4 (owner): the phone no longer shrinks at all when it goes aside. Kept as a
       * token rather than deleted because the geometry below is expressed in terms of it. */
      --aside-scale: 1;

      /* How much of the phone ends up past the right edge - 30% at round 1, cut to 10% at
       * round 4 so less of the screen is whitespace. */
      --aside-fraction: 0.1;

      /* The move that puts exactly `--aside-fraction` of the phone past the right edge. Its
       * centre starts at 50vw and must land at `100vw - (0.5 - f) * w`. Derived from the
       * fraction rather than dialled in, so changing the overhang is one number and the
       * date column below follows it automatically. */
      --aside-x: calc(50vw - (0.5 - var(--aside-fraction)) * var(--phone-w) * var(--aside-scale));

      /* Where the phone's left edge lands once aside - what the date column butts against. */
      --phone-left: calc(100vw - (1 - var(--aside-fraction)) * var(--phone-w) * var(--aside-scale));

      /* The scroll the phone spends moving aside once it has pinned. */
      --aside-span: 34svh;

      /* One release per step down the date column - the section's own scroll rhythm. 62svh
       * until the closing round (owner: the second swipe "even earlier ... so that the third
       * feed item is visible for a lot longer"). */
      --date-step: 40svh;
      /* What one date block occupies, measured - the calendar rule spans the gap between
       * two of them, so it needs to know. */
      --stamp-h: 46px;
      /*
       * Where the first date sits, in the pin's coordinates. The pager advances once the
       * phone's centre is `0.5 + HYSTERESIS` (0.56) of a step past a date's centre, and the
       * phone's centre is half its height below its top, which is `stuck` px down the pin.
       * Closing round (owner): the first date's centre sits that threshold short of the
       * phone's centre at the end of the move, which is also how `promise.ts` reads the move's
       * length back. (The first swipe itself left this position at 8.14 - it now starts 10% into
       * the move - but every later swipe is still timed from this column.) (It used to be `50svh + --aside-span`, which assumed the phone's centre sat
       * at mid-screen in the pin's coordinates and put the first swipe ~0.56 of a step plus
       * ~116px after the move.) `promise.ts` derives the aside span back from this position.
